A = {
"d-malinche": dict(
 bluf="Malinche's languages let Cortés negotiate, recruit allies and read Aztec politics. Without her, his small force could not have built the coalition that toppled the empire. She also became Mexico's most contested symbol of the conquest.",
 why="Cortés needed to talk to the Aztecs and their rivals. She spoke Nahuatl and Maya, soon learned Spanish, and understood the region's politics.",
 then="She interpreted at every major encounter, including with Moctezuma, and bore Cortés a son, Martín, one of the first prominent mestizos.",
 now="In Mexico 'malinchista' means someone who prefers foreign things. Many historians now see her as an enslaved woman surviving impossible choices rather than a traitor.",
 lens="Interpreters and go-betweens hold quiet power in any encounter between cultures: whoever controls translation shapes what each side understands."),
"as15208": dict(
 bluf="Suleiman's 46-year reign was the peak of Ottoman power. The empire reached Hungary, Baghdad and North Africa, rivalled the Habsburgs, and built legal and administrative institutions that lasted centuries. It was also the point where expansion hit its geographic limits.",
 story="Suleiman took Belgrade (1521), Rhodes (1522) and most of Hungary after Mohács (1526), besieged Vienna (1529), captured Baghdad (1534) and built a navy that dominated the Mediterranean. At home his law codes earned him the title 'the Lawgiver', and his architect Sinan built mosques that still define Istanbul's skyline.",
 why="He inherited a full treasury and an army strengthened by his father's conquest of Egypt and Syria, and a state built to expand: conquest rewarded soldiers and officials with land revenues.",
 then="By his death the empire faced long, costly fronts against the Habsburgs in Hungary, the Safavids in Iraq and Iran, and Portugal in the Indian Ocean. Expansion slowed, and power shifted from sultans to grand viziers and palace factions. He also allied with France against the Habsburgs (1536), an early case of interest overriding religion in European diplomacy.",
 now="Turkey remembers his reign as its golden age, celebrated in popular TV dramas. Ottoman law and administration shaped the Balkans and the Arab Middle East for centuries.",
 lens="Empires that reward their elites with conquests run into trouble when expansion reaches its natural limits."),
"d-noche": dict(
 bluf="The Aztec rising of June 1520 showed that the invaders could be beaten on Aztec ground. Cortés survived only because his Tlaxcalan allies stayed loyal, and the smallpox epidemic that followed shifted the balance back.",
 why="Pedro de Alvarado's massacre of Aztec nobles at the festival of Toxcatl provoked a rising, and Moctezuma's death in Spanish custody left the Spaniards without a hostage to bargain with.",
 then="Cortés retreated to Tlaxcala, rebuilt with reinforcements and Indigenous allies, and built boats for a siege. Smallpox killed Moctezuma's successor Cuitláhuac and many of the city's defenders.",
 now="In 2021 Mexico City renamed the tree linked to the episode from the 'Noche Triste' (sad night) to the 'Noche Victoriosa' (victorious night), a sign of how national memory has shifted toward the Indigenous side.",
 lens="One battlefield defeat rarely decides a war if the loser keeps its alliances and the winner is weakened by other forces."),
"eu152114": dict(
 bluf="Luther's refusal to recant before the emperor turned a theological dispute into a political crisis. Charles V outlawed him, but German princes protected him, showing that the empire's fragmented structure would let the Reformation survive.",
 why="Charles V wanted religious unity in his empire, but he needed the German princes' support against France and the Ottomans, so he could not crush Luther's protectors.",
 then="The Edict of Worms made Luther an outlaw, but Frederick the Wise of Saxony hid him at the Wartburg. The edict was never enforced, and many princes and cities adopted Lutheranism in the 1520s.",
 now="'Here I stand' became a byword for conscience against authority, and Worms is a founding moment in the history of freedom of conscience.",
 lens="Decrees matter only as far as someone will enforce them. In divided states, dissidents survive by finding protectors."),
"la15215": dict(
 bluf="The fall of Tenochtitlan ended the Aztec Empire and made central Mexico the core of Spain's American empire. It was won by an Indigenous coalition under Spanish leadership, and by epidemic disease, which together destroyed the largest city in the Americas.",
 story="Cortés returned in 1521 with Spanish reinforcements, tens of thousands of Tlaxcalan and other Indigenous allies, and brigantines built to control the lake. Smallpox had already killed huge numbers, including the emperor Cuitláhuac. After a 75-day siege, the last emperor, Cuauhtémoc, was captured on August 13, 1521, and the city was destroyed block by block.",
 why="Aztec rule rested on tribute and fear rather than integration, so subject and rival peoples joined the invaders. Smallpox, to which Native Americans had no immunity, devastated the defenders.",
 then="Spain built Mexico City on the ruins and ran New Spain through the existing Indigenous nobility and tribute systems. Disease and forced labor cut central Mexico's Indigenous population, by most estimates, by 80-90% within a century. Tlaxcala kept special privileges as a reward for its alliance.",
 now="Mexican national identity is built on this collision: the Aztec eagle on the flag, the Spanish language and Catholic faith, and a mestizo majority. Mexico City still sinks into the drained lakebed.",
 lens="Empires ruled by tribute and fear are brittle: when an outsider appears, subject peoples may see liberation from their old masters rather than invasion."),
"d-lutherbible": dict(
 bluf="Luther's New Testament put scripture into the language of ordinary Germans, the practical engine of the Reformation's appeal to individual reading. It also helped create a standard written German that tied together a politically divided people.",
 why="Luther believed every Christian should read the Bible directly, without depending on priests or Latin, and printing made a cheap vernacular Bible possible.",
 then="The 'September Testament' sold out quickly, and the full Bible (1534) went through many editions. Protestant regions stressed reading, which raised literacy.",
 now="Modern standard German owes much to Luther's Bible. Protestant regions had higher literacy for centuries, a gap economists still study.",
 lens="Translating a sacred or legal text into everyday language shifts power from the interpreters to the readers."),
"af15267": dict(
 bluf="Afonso's letters show an African state that adopted Christianity and dealt with Portugal as a diplomatic equal, yet could not control the slave trade Portuguese merchants drove. They are early evidence that the trade undermined African states from within.",
 story="Afonso I (Nzinga Mbemba) made Christianity Kongo's state religion and exchanged envoys with Lisbon and Rome. But Portuguese traders based on São Tomé were buying and seizing his subjects, even nobles, paying with goods that let provincial chiefs bypass the king. In 1526 he wrote to King João III asking him to stop it.",
 why="The trade enriched provincial lords and Portuguese merchants beyond royal control, undermining the king's authority and draining his population.",
 then="Afonso set up royal inspection of slave sales rather than ending them, and Portugal ignored his pleas. Kongo was weakened by slaving and succession wars; Portugal later founded Angola (1575) to the south as a slaving base and defeated Kongo at Mbwila in 1665.",
 now="His letters are among the earliest African written protests against the slave trade. They complicate any story of Africans as either passive victims or willing partners.",
 lens="New commerce that bypasses a central authority empowers local middlemen and weakens the state. Watch who profits."),
"as15269": dict(
 bluf="Babur's victory at Panipat founded the Mughal Empire, which unified most of South Asia and became one of the richest states in the world. Its blend of Persian, Central Asian and Indian culture shaped the subcontinent's languages, architecture and politics.",
 story="Babur, a Timurid prince descended from Timur and Genghis Khan, lost his Central Asian homeland to the Uzbeks and turned south from Kabul. At Panipat in 1526 his field guns and mounted archers defeated the far larger army of Ibrahim Lodi, sultan of Delhi; a year later he beat a Rajput confederacy at Khanwa.",
 why="Driven out of Central Asia, Babur needed a new kingdom. The Delhi Sultanate was split by feuds among its Afghan nobles, some of whom invited him in.",
 then="His son Humayun lost the throne to Sher Shah Suri and regained it only in 1555; his grandson Akbar built the lasting empire. Mughal rule integrated North India's administration, tax system and court culture.",
 now="Urdu, Mughal architecture such as the Taj Mahal, and much of North Indian cuisine come from this era. How to remember Muslim rulers is a live issue in Indian politics today.",
 lens="New military technology plus local allies lets small invading forces found large states, but holding them requires accommodating the conquered."),
"eu152615": dict(
 bluf="Mohács destroyed the independent Kingdom of Hungary and brought the Ottoman and Habsburg empires face to face in Central Europe. It made the Habsburgs the front-line defenders of Christian Europe and gave them the Hungarian crown.",
 why="Suleiman sought to expand into Central Europe and weaken the Habsburgs. Hungary was divided among feuding nobles and received little help from the rest of Europe.",
 then="Louis II died without heirs, so Ferdinand of Habsburg claimed the throne. Hungary was split three ways for 150 years: Habsburg 'Royal Hungary', Ottoman central Hungary, and the Ottoman vassal principality of Transylvania.",
 now="Mohács is a national trauma in Hungary, and the long Habsburg connection and Ottoman frontier still shape Central European identity.",
 lens="Small kingdoms between two great empires usually lose their independence when their own elites are divided."),
"af15298": dict(
 bluf="The war between the Adal Sultanate and Christian Ethiopia nearly destroyed the Ethiopian kingdom and pulled the Ottomans and Portuguese into the Horn of Africa. Local religious and trade rivalries became part of the global Ottoman-Portuguese contest.",
 why="Adal and Ethiopia had long competed over trade routes to the Red Sea and over highland territory. Ottoman firearms gave Adal a decisive edge.",
 then="With help from about 400 Portuguese musketeers, Ethiopian forces killed Imam Ahmad at Wayna Daga in 1543. Both sides were exhausted, which allowed Oromo pastoralists to migrate into large parts of the highlands and lowlands.",
 now="Ethiopia's Christian identity endured. The war still colours memory between Ethiopians and Somalis, many of whom regard Imam Ahmad as a hero.",
 lens="Regional rivals pull global powers in. Watch for outsiders arming local sides."),
"eu152916": dict(
 bluf="The failed siege of 1529 marked the practical limit of Ottoman expansion into Central Europe. Distance decided it: Vienna lay at the edge of how far an army marching from Istanbul could fight in one season.",
 why="Suleiman aimed to oust Ferdinand of Habsburg, his rival for Hungary, and backed a rival Hungarian king, John Zápolya.",
 then="The Ottomans held central Hungary but never took Vienna. The two empires settled into a long frontier war, and the Habsburgs built a fortified military frontier.",
 now="The sieges of 1529 and 1683 made Vienna the 'gate' of Christian Europe in popular memory, an image still used in debates about Europe's borders and identity.",
 lens="Logistics sets the limits of empire: armies that must march from a distant capital each season can only reach so far."),
"d-incacivil": dict(
 bluf="The war between Atahualpa and Huáscar split the Inca state just as the Spanish arrived. Pizarro could exploit the factions, and some Andean peoples saw the newcomers as allies against the victors.",
 why="Inca succession had no fixed rule, and an epidemic, probably smallpox spreading ahead of the Spanish, killed the emperor Huayna Capac and his chosen heir around 1527.",
 then="Atahualpa's armies won, but Huáscar's supporters in Cusco and conquered peoples such as the Cañari and Chachapoya resented the victors, and some later joined the Spanish.",
 now="Many historians see the civil war, together with disease, as a key reason a few hundred Spaniards could topple the empire.",
 lens="Succession crises are the most dangerous moments for personal rule, and outsiders exploit them."),
"la15326": dict(
 bluf="Pizarro's capture of Atahualpa decapitated the Inca state, the largest empire in the Americas, and opened the Andes to Spanish conquest. Andean silver, soon found at Potosí, would finance Spain and knit together the world economy.",
 story="In November 1532 at Cajamarca, about 170 Spaniards ambushed Atahualpa's unarmed retinue with guns, horses and steel, and captured him. He paid a room full of gold and silver for his release, but Pizarro executed him in 1533 and marched on Cusco with Indigenous allies.",
 why="Smallpox and a civil war had weakened the empire, and its highly centralized structure meant that capturing the ruler paralysed command.",
 trig="Atahualpa agreed to meet the Spaniards in Cajamarca's square, confident in the army of tens of thousands camped nearby.",
 then="Spain installed puppet Inca rulers, faced a great rebellion in 1536 and fought bitter wars among the conquerors themselves; an Inca state held out in Vilcabamba until 1572. Spain founded Lima and built the Viceroyalty of Peru on Andean labor systems.",
 now="Millions of people in Peru, Bolivia and Ecuador still speak Quechua, and Indigenous movements in the Andes frame politics in terms of the conquest.",
 lens="The more centralized a state, the more vulnerable it is to a blow at the top."),
"eu153417": dict(
 bluf="Henry VIII's break with Rome put the English church under the crown and transferred huge wealth from the monasteries to the king and gentry. It made religion a matter of national sovereignty and set England on a Protestant path that defined its identity and rivalries for centuries.",
 why="Henry needed a male heir to secure the young Tudor dynasty. Pope Clement VII would not annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon, partly because her nephew Charles V controlled Rome. Anticlerical feeling and new Protestant ideas gave the king allies.",
 trig="Henry married Anne Boleyn in 1533, and Parliament passed the Act of Supremacy in 1534.",
 then="The dissolution of the monasteries (1536-41) sold church land to the gentry, giving a powerful class a stake in the break. England swung between Protestant and Catholic under Edward VI and Mary I until Elizabeth's settlement, and Catholicism became associated with foreign enemies such as Spain.",
 now="The monarch is still Supreme Governor of the Church of England, and the worldwide Anglican Communion counts about 85 million members. Anti-Catholicism shaped British identity and the conflict in Ireland.",
 lens="A break with an international authority sticks when powerful groups at home profit from it."),
"na15341": dict(
 bluf="Cartier's voyages gave France its claim to the St. Lawrence, the river system leading into the heart of North America. That route became the backbone of New France and its fur trade with Indigenous nations.",
 why="Francis I wanted a share of the Americas, rejecting the Spanish-Portuguese division of the world, and hoped for a Northwest Passage to Asia and for gold.",
 then="Cartier's attempts at settlement failed, but the claim held; Champlain founded Quebec on the same river in 1608.",
 now="The name 'Canada', from an Iroquoian word for village, and the French-speaking society of Quebec both trace back to this route.",
 lens="Rivers decide where empires go: in pre-industrial North America, waterways determined who could reach the interior."),
"d-lima": dict(
 bluf="Founding Lima on the coast turned the Andes toward the sea and Spain. Lima became the capital of a viceroyalty governing most of Spanish South America, and Peru's centre of power moved from the Indigenous highlands to a Spanish coastal city.",
 why="A coastal capital linked the colony to Panama and Spain, unlike Cusco high in the Andes, and was safer from Inca resistance.",
 then="Lima became home to the viceroy, the Inquisition, the merchants who controlled silver exports, and the University of San Marcos (1551), one of the oldest in the Americas.",
 now="Peru's sharp divide between a dominant coastal capital and the Andean and Amazonian interior, visible in its election maps, goes back to this choice.",
 lens="Where a conqueror puts the capital reveals whose interests the state will serve."),
"d-jesuits": dict(
 bluf="The Jesuits gave the Counter-Reformation its most effective force: disciplined, highly educated priests who ran schools, advised rulers and carried Catholicism to Asia and the Americas. They made education a weapon of religious competition.",
 why="The Church needed educated clergy to answer the Protestant challenge. Ignatius, a former soldier, offered an order bound by obedience and rigorous training.",
 then="Jesuit schools educated Catholic elites across Europe; missionaries such as Francis Xavier and Matteo Ricci reached Japan and China by adapting to local cultures. Their power and wealth made enemies, and the order was suppressed in 1773 and restored in 1814.",
 now="Jesuit universities such as Georgetown and Sophia in Tokyo remain influential, and Pope Francis (2013-25) was the first Jesuit pope.",
 lens="Movements that control education shape elites for generations."),
"as154310": dict(
 bluf="Firearms transformed Japan's civil wars: within decades massed musketeers decided battles, and the warlords who could afford them unified the country. Japan's speed in copying and mass-producing the weapon shows how fast a society under competitive pressure can absorb foreign technology.",
 why="Japan's warring lords had every incentive to adopt a military advantage, and skilled swordsmiths could copy the guns.",
 then="Gun production spread across Japan, and Oda Nobunaga used massed volleys at Nagashino (1575). Portuguese traders and Jesuit missionaries followed. After unification, the Tokugawa restricted both guns and foreign contact.",
 now="Japan's rapid adoption of guns in the 1500s, and of Western industry after 1868, contradicts the stereotype of a closed society resistant to change.",
 lens="Competition drives adoption: states under pressure copy foreign technology fastest."),
"d-vesalius": dict(
 bluf="Vesalius showed that direct observation could overturn ancient authority, starting with the human body. His anatomy made medicine an empirical science and belongs to the same 1543 moment as Copernicus.",
 why="Renaissance scholars recovering ancient texts were finding contradictions in them. Access to dissection at Padua, plus skilled printing and woodcuts, made a precise illustrated anatomy possible.",
 then="Anatomy became a science based on dissection, and Padua trained a generation of doctors, including William Harvey, who described the circulation of the blood in 1628.",
 now="Evidence-based medicine rests on the principle he championed: trust what you observe over inherited authority.",
 lens="Authority based on ancient texts falls when someone checks the evidence and publishes it widely."),
"eu154318": dict(
 bluf="Copernicus put the Sun, not the Earth, at the centre of the cosmos. His model challenged both ancient science and the Church's picture of the world, and launched the Scientific Revolution that culminated with Newton.",
 why="Ptolemy's Earth-centred system needed ever more complicated adjustments to predict planetary positions. Renaissance access to Greek texts, and the Church's own need for an accurate calendar, encouraged new models.",
 then="Few accepted it at first, since it predicted positions no better than the old system. Kepler's elliptical orbits and Galileo's telescope made the case; the Church banned Copernican works in 1616 and tried Galileo in 1633.",
 now="Science's claim to challenge received beliefs, and the recurring conflicts between scientific findings and religious or political authority, start here.",
 lens="Big shifts in thinking take generations: new ideas often win when their opponents age out, not when they are first proven."),
"eu154519": dict(
 bluf="Trent was Catholicism's answer to the Reformation: it reformed abuses but refused to compromise on doctrine. It fixed the line between Catholic and Protestant Europe and created the disciplined, centralized Church that lasted until the 1960s.",
 why="Protestant gains in Germany, Scandinavia and England, and long-standing complaints about corruption and poorly trained priests, forced Rome to act. Rivalry between pope and emperor delayed the council for years.",
 then="It confirmed the seven sacraments, the authority of tradition and the Latin Vulgate Bible, ordered seminaries to train priests, and backed the Index of Forbidden Books. The Counter-Reformation used the Jesuits, the Inquisition and Baroque art to win back Poland, Austria and southern Germany.",
 now="The Latin 'Tridentine' Mass and Trent's Church structure lasted until the Second Vatican Council (1962-65). Catholic traditionalists and reformers still argue over its legacy.",
 lens="Institutions under attack often reform their practices while hardening their core claims."),
"la15457": dict(
 bluf="Potosí's silver made Spain the first global empire and created the first truly global trade, as American silver flowed to Europe and on to China. It was dug by forced Indigenous labor that devastated Andean communities.",
 story="In 1545 silver was found at the Cerro Rico ('rich hill') in the Andes of today's Bolivia. Potosí quickly grew to well over 100,000 people, one of the largest cities in the Americas. From the 1570s Viceroy Toledo organized the mita, a forced labor draft that sent Indigenous men to the mines, and a mercury refining process greatly increased output.",
 why="Spain's hunger for revenue and China's demand for silver made the mine immensely valuable, and the colonial state could compel Andean labor to work it.",
 then="Silver paid for Spain's wars but also fed inflation across Europe (the 'price revolution') and made Spain dependent on bullion rather than manufacturing. Through the Manila galleons, much of it ended up in Ming China, which had shifted its taxes to silver.",
 now="The Spanish silver peso became the first global currency and a model for the US dollar. Bolivia remains one of South America's poorest countries, a classic case of the 'resource curse'.",
 lens="Resource windfalls can weaken the economy that receives them. Follow the money to see who builds industry and who simply spends."),
"eu155520": dict(
 bluf="The Peace of Augsburg accepted that the empire's religious unity was gone and let each prince choose his territory's faith. It replaced the idea of one Christian truth with a political deal, but by excluding Calvinists it set up the Thirty Years' War.",
 why="Neither side could win: Charles V defeated the Lutheran princes in 1547, but a princes' revolt in 1552 reversed his victory.",
 then="Religion became a matter of territorial sovereignty, and subjects who disagreed could emigrate. The spread of Calvinism and disputes over church lands undermined the settlement.",
 now="'Whose realm, his religion' is a milestone in the shift from religious to state authority in Europe and a step toward the system of sovereign states fixed in 1648.",
 lens="Compromises that leave out a rising group store up the next conflict."),
"as155611": dict(
 bluf="Akbar turned the Mughal conquest into a durable empire by bringing Hindu Rajput elites into government, building a centralized tax system and practising religious tolerance. His model of accommodation kept the Mughals dominant in South Asia for 150 years.",
 why="The Mughals were a small Muslim ruling group over a mostly Hindu population. Lasting rule required local allies and a reliable tax base.",
 then="He abolished the jizya tax on non-Muslims (1564), made Rajput rulers his generals and in-laws, created a ranked imperial nobility, and surveyed land to set taxes. Aurangzeb later reversed much of this, which helped provoke revolts that weakened the empire.",
 now="Akbar is a symbol of pluralism in India's debates between secularists and Hindu nationalists.",
 lens="Conquerors who co-opt local elites rule longer than those who rely on force or religious exclusion."),
"eu155821": dict(
 bluf="Elizabeth's 45-year reign settled England as a Protestant state, kept it independent of Catholic Spain, and began its turn toward the sea and overseas expansion. Her moderate religious settlement held the country together after decades of upheaval.",
 why="Her Catholic half-sister Mary I died childless, leaving Elizabeth heir under Henry VIII's will.",
 then="Her 1559 settlement created a broad Protestant church. She backed Dutch rebels and privateers against Spain, survived the Armada, and chartered the East India Company (1600). Because she never married, the throne passed to the Scottish Stuarts.",
 now="The Anglican church, the beginnings of English sea power and the East India Company date from her reign, and the 'Elizabethan golden age' is central to English national identity.",
 lens="Leaders who avoid forcing religious or ideological purity often hold divided countries together."),
"eu156222": dict(
 bluf="France's 36 years of civil war showed how religious division, a weak monarchy and aristocratic factions could tear apart Europe's most populous kingdom. Its resolution pointed toward a state that put political order above religious unity.",
 why="Calvinism spread fast among nobles and townspeople. After Henry II died in 1559, his young sons could not control rival noble families (Guise, Bourbon, Montmorency), who used religion to rally followers.",
 trig="Men of the Duke of Guise massacred Protestant worshippers at Vassy in March 1562.",
 then="Henry IV, a Protestant heir, converted to Catholicism to win Paris and issued the Edict of Nantes (1598), granting the Huguenots toleration. Exhaustion produced the 'politiques', who argued that the state's survival mattered more than religious uniformity.",
 now="France's tradition of a strong central state, and its suspicion of religion in public life, owe something to the memory of these wars.",
 lens="Religious wars are often elite power struggles in religious dress: ask who leads each side and what they stand to gain."),
"la15658": dict(
 bluf="The Manila galleons linked the Americas to Asia and completed the first global trade network. American silver bought Chinese silk and porcelain, tying Ming China's economy to Spanish American mines.",
 why="China's economy had shifted to silver for taxes and trade, so silver was worth far more there than in Europe. Spain needed a way to reach Asian markets from its American colonies.",
 then="Silver flowed to China for 250 years. American crops such as maize, sweet potatoes and chillies spread to Asia, helping China's population grow, and Chinese merchants settled in Manila.",
 now="The galleon trade is often called the beginning of globalization. Culinary and cultural links between Mexico and the Philippines, and the Chinese communities of Southeast Asia, trace back to it.",
 lens="Trade follows price gaps: when a good is worth much more in one place than another, merchants will find a way to connect them."),
"sa-talikota": dict(
 bluf="Talikota destroyed Vijayanagara, the last great Hindu empire of South India, and its capital Hampi. The power vacuum fragmented the Deccan and later opened it to Mughal and then European expansion.",
 why="The Deccan sultanates and Vijayanagara competed over the fertile Raichur Doab. Rama Raya's habit of playing the sultanates off against one another finally united them against him.",
 then="A smaller Vijayanagara state survived further south while its local governors (nayakas) became independent. The sultanates fought each other and were conquered by the Mughals in the late 1600s.",
 now="Hampi's ruins are a UNESCO site, and Vijayanagara is invoked in Indian politics as a symbol of Hindu resistance, though its armies included Muslim soldiers and it allied with sultanates when convenient.",
 lens="Playing rivals against each other works until it convinces them to unite."),
"eu156823": dict(
 bluf="The Dutch Revolt created a new kind of state: a decentralized, commercial republic that tolerated religious diversity and became the world's leading trading power. It showed that a small country with capital and sea power could defy Europe's largest empire.",
 why="Philip II's heavy taxes, his centralization that overrode local privileges, and his persecution of Protestants alienated both the nobles and the towns of the wealthy Low Countries.",
 trig="The Duke of Alba's brutal repression after 1567, including the execution of leading nobles, turned protest into armed revolt in 1568.",
 then="The northern provinces declared independence in 1581 and won recognition in 1648; the south stayed Spanish and became today's Belgium. The Dutch Republic built modern financial tools, including a stock exchange and a public exchange bank, and a global trading empire.",
 now="The Netherlands-Belgium split, Dutch traditions of tolerance and trade, and many basic tools of modern capitalism trace back to the revolt.",
 lens="Rulers who override local privileges to raise taxes meet the fiercest resistance in their richest, best-organized regions."),
"as157112": dict(
 bluf="Manila's founding gave Spain its Asian base and the Philippines its colonial capital, linking Chinese traders with American silver. Spanish rule made the Philippines Asia's leading Catholic nation.",
 why="Spain wanted a base near China and the Spice Islands. Manila had an excellent harbour, held by Muslim rulers such as Rajah Sulayman.",
 then="Manila became the hub of the galleon trade, and Chinese merchants settled there in large numbers, though they were periodically expelled or massacred. Spain ruled the islands for 327 years, until 1898.",
 now="About 80% of Filipinos are Catholic. Spanish names and loanwords, and the separate identity of the Muslim south, show where Spanish rule did and did not reach.",
 lens="The places colonial power never fully controlled often mark a country's internal divides today: compare the Catholic north and Muslim Mindanao."),
"eu157124": dict(
 bluf="Lepanto was a heavy Ottoman defeat at sea and a huge morale boost for Catholic Europe, but its strategic effect was limited: the Ottomans rebuilt their fleet within a year and kept Cyprus. It marked the end of Ottoman naval expansion in the western Mediterranean.",
 why="The Ottomans seized Venetian Cyprus in 1570, and Pope Pius V formed a Holy League with Spain and Venice.",
 then="Venice made a separate peace in 1573, giving up Cyprus, and Spain and the Ottomans turned to other fronts. The Mediterranean settled into stalemate, while the Atlantic became Europe's main arena.",
 now="Lepanto remains a symbol in narratives about defending Christian Europe, usually overstated compared with its strategic effect.",
 lens="Separate symbolic victories from strategic ones: ask what the loser could rebuild and what the winner actually gained."),
"eu157225": dict(
 bluf="The massacre of Huguenot leaders and thousands of Protestants destroyed any hope of compromise in France's religious wars. It radicalized Protestants, who began to argue that subjects could resist a tyrannical king.",
 why="Catholic leaders feared the Huguenot admiral Coligny's influence over the young Charles IX and his push for war with Spain. The wedding of the Protestant Henry of Navarre to the king's sister had brought the Huguenot leadership to Paris.",
 then="Killings spread from Paris to provincial cities, leaving thousands dead. Huguenot writers developed theories of resistance and limited monarchy, and the wars dragged on for 26 more years.",
 now="It is one of Europe's most notorious episodes of religious violence, and the resistance theories it provoked fed later ideas of constitutional government.",
 lens="Atrocities meant to end a conflict often radicalize the other side instead."),
"la15729": dict(
 bluf="The capture and execution of Túpac Amaru ended the last independent Inca state and completed Spain's conquest of the Andes. Viceroy Toledo then reorganized Andean society to extract silver and tribute more efficiently.",
 why="Toledo wanted to end the last independent Inca state and tighten Spanish control over Andean labor.",
 then="Toledo forced Indigenous people into new planned towns (reducciones), formalized the mita labor draft for Potosí and cracked down on the Inca nobility. Resistance went underground and resurfaced in later rebellions.",
 now="Túpac Amaru's name became a symbol of resistance, taken up by the great rebellion of 1780, by guerrilla movements and even by the rapper Tupac Shakur.",
 lens="Conquests are completed less by battles than by reorganizing how people live, work and pay."),
"d-tycho": dict(
 bluf="Tycho's measurements of the 1572 supernova showed that the heavens could change, contradicting Aristotle's teaching that everything beyond the Moon was perfect and unchanging. His later precise observations gave Kepler the data to work out how planets move.",
 why="A rare exploding star appeared just when an astronomer obsessed with precise measurement was watching.",
 then="Denmark's king funded Tycho's observatory on Hven. His data, inherited by Kepler in 1601, led to the laws of planetary motion.",
 now="Science in which careful measurement tests theory owes much to Tycho's method.",
 lens="Better measurement often comes before better theory: those who collect reliable data make breakthroughs possible."),
"d-akbar-ibadat": dict(
 bluf="Akbar's House of Worship turned religious debate into imperial policy: the ruler of a diverse empire set himself above any single faith. The 'peace with all' that followed underpinned Mughal rule over a Hindu-majority population.",
 why="Akbar ruled a mostly non-Muslim empire, wanted to curb orthodox clerics' influence over the throne, and was personally curious about faith.",
 then="In 1579 he claimed the authority to rule on religious disputes, and his policy of sulh-i kul ('peace with all') guided appointments and law. Orthodox clerics resented it, and later emperors moved back toward Islamic orthodoxy.",
 now="His court is cited as a model of pluralism in South Asia's debates over secularism.",
 lens="Rulers of diverse empires often place themselves above all faiths to limit the power of any one religious establishment."),
"na15872": dict(
 bluf="Roanoke's failure taught England that colonies could not survive as private adventures without steady supplies, money and workable relations with Native peoples. Those lessons shaped the joint-stock company model behind Jamestown.",
 why="Sir Walter Raleigh, holding a royal patent, wanted a base for raiding Spanish shipping and a claim to North American land.",
 then="War with Spain delayed resupply until 1590, by which time the colony was gone. England's next attempt, Jamestown (1607), was organized by a joint-stock company with far more capital.",
 now="The 'Lost Colony' remains unsolved; many historians think survivors joined nearby Native communities such as the Croatoan.",
 lens="Distant outposts live on their supply lines; when the home country is distracted by war, they wither."),
"d-maryqos": dict(
 bluf="Mary's execution removed the Catholic claimant around whom plots against Elizabeth gathered, and pushed Philip II to invade England directly. It also cleared the way for her Protestant son to unite the crowns of Scotland and England.",
 why="Catholic plotters, backed by Spain, saw Mary as Elizabeth's rightful heir, and the Babington Plot gave Elizabeth's ministers the evidence to convict her.",
 then="Philip II pressed ahead with the Armada, now claiming England for himself. In 1603 her son James VI of Scotland became James I of England.",
 now="The union of crowns led to the union of England and Scotland in 1707; the debate over Scotland's place in the United Kingdom continues.",
 lens="A rival claimant in custody is a permanent focus for conspiracy, and regimes eventually choose between that risk and execution."),
"d-cadiz": dict(
 bluf="Drake's raid on Cádiz delayed the Armada by a year and spoiled its supplies. It showed the value of striking an enemy's forces before they can sail.",
 why="Elizabeth's government wanted to wreck Philip II's invasion plans before they were ready.",
 then="The Armada sailed in 1588, a year late, with barrels of unseasoned wood that leaked and spoiled its food and water.",
 now="It remains a textbook case of a pre-emptive naval strike.",
 lens="Logistics is a target: destroying supplies can matter as much as sinking warships."),
"eu158826": dict(
 bluf="The Armada's defeat saved Protestant England from invasion and kept the Dutch Revolt alive, checking Spain's bid to dominate northern Europe. It did not end Spanish power, but it began England's long rise as a naval and later imperial power.",
 story="In 1588 Philip II sent about 130 ships up the Channel to escort the Duke of Parma's army from the Netherlands across to England. English ships harried it, fire ships broke its formation off Calais, and the battle of Gravelines scattered it. Storms wrecked dozens more as it sailed home around Scotland and Ireland.",
 why="Philip wanted to restore Catholicism in England, end English support for the Dutch rebels and stop English raids on Spanish treasure fleets.",
 trig="The execution of Mary, Queen of Scots in 1587 removed his hope of a Catholic successor and freed him to claim England for himself.",
 then="The war dragged on until 1604 and Spain rebuilt its navy, but England and the Dutch kept their independence and expanded into the Atlantic and Asia. Protestantism became central to English national identity.",
 now="The Armada is a founding myth of British sea power and of England as a nation defined against Catholic continental powers.",
 lens="Invading an island requires controlling the sea first. That logic shaped British strategy from 1588 to 1940."),
"as159013": dict(
 bluf="Hideyoshi completed the unification of Japan begun by Nobunaga and froze its social order, disarming peasants and separating samurai from farmers. His failed invasions of Korea devastated the peninsula and left lasting bitterness.",
 why="He took over Nobunaga's armies and alliances after Nobunaga's assassination in 1582, and combined force with deals that let defeated lords keep their domains in return for loyalty.",
 then="His 'sword hunt' (1588) disarmed the peasantry, his edicts fixed social status, and a national land survey standardized taxes. He invaded Korea in 1592 and 1597 and failed. After his death in 1598, Tokugawa Ieyasu won the struggle for power.",
 now="The invasions are still remembered in Korea, where Admiral Yi Sun-sin is a national hero, and Hideyoshi's social order became the base of the Tokugawa system.",
 lens="Unifiers often turn their armies abroad once the wars at home end, to keep soldiers and lords occupied."),
}
